1
00:00:02,200 --> 00:00:03,147
Jezero Crater:

2
00:00:03,197 --> 00:00:06,600
an ancient lake bed on Mars, where NASA's
Perseverance rover is searching for signs of

3
00:00:06,650 --> 00:00:10,053
past life.

4
00:00:10,103 --> 00:00:12,973
Now imagine planning your own walk there.

5
00:00:13,877 --> 00:00:15,630
We're Binary Explorers.

6
00:00:15,680 --> 00:00:20,333
Imam Hosen, our team lead, architected and
built the system.

7
00:00:20,383 --> 00:00:24,408
Ahad, our NASA data analyst, verified every
dataset.

8
00:00:24,458 --> 00:00:31,384
And Biswadev, our 3D designer and QA tester,
shaped the visuals and tested every feature.

9
00:00:32,288 --> 00:00:35,337
We love Mars, and we kept hitting the same
wall:

10
00:00:35,387 --> 00:00:39,986
the data is public, but the tools rarely show
where an answer came from.

11
00:00:40,036 --> 00:00:42,117
So we built one that always does.

12
00:00:44,021 --> 00:00:47,986
NASA publishes thirteen hundred Mars datasets,
elevation down to one metre, and the rover's

13
00:00:48,036 --> 00:00:52,001
path sol by sol.

14
00:00:52,051 --> 00:00:52,819
All open...

15
00:00:52,869 --> 00:00:53,860
but scattered.

16
00:00:54,765 --> 00:00:55,732
Who feels that?

17
00:00:55,782 --> 00:00:58,376
A student asking why Jezero was chosen.

18
00:00:58,426 --> 00:01:01,360
A teacher who wants a real mission in class.

19
00:01:01,410 --> 00:01:04,140
One day, the first crews to walk on Mars.

20
00:01:04,190 --> 00:01:08,276
And when a chatbot invents a coordinate, no
one can check it.

21
00:01:09,180 --> 00:01:12,126
Signals take up to twenty-two minutes between
Earth and Mars, so crews must plan on their

22
00:01:12,176 --> 00:01:15,121
own.

23
00:01:15,171 --> 00:01:16,141
The opportunity:

24
00:01:16,191 --> 00:01:19,964
NASA's evidence, and its limits, in one place
you can trace.

25
00:01:21,869 --> 00:01:22,814
Our big idea:

26
00:01:22,864 --> 00:01:26,030
a Martian map built only on NASA evidence.

27
00:01:26,080 --> 00:01:31,198
Switch Trek layers, scrub Perseverance sol by
sol, and add official feature names and HiRISE

28
00:01:31,248 --> 00:01:36,366
terrain models from data dot NASA dot gov.

29
00:01:37,270 --> 00:01:42,653
The Left on Mars layer shows every lander and
rover NASA has operated on Mars.

30
00:01:42,703 --> 00:01:47,459
Drag the timeline from 1976 to today, and
watch each one fall silent.

31
00:01:48,363 --> 00:01:51,580
Draw a Marswalk, or load Perseverance's real
route.

32
00:01:51,630 --> 00:01:56,833
The terrain is sampled from NASA's elevation
model, every Risk Index component cites its

33
00:01:56,883 --> 00:02:02,086
source, and the cockpit names the craters and
terrain models along the way.

34
00:02:02,991 --> 00:02:07,588
Pick two points for a slope-aware A* corridor,
always non-certifying, then fly it in 3D over

35
00:02:07,638 --> 00:02:12,235
HiRISE imagery.

36
00:02:13,139 --> 00:02:17,852
Presets, Classroom Mode, a command palette,
focus mode and share links are one click away,

37
00:02:17,902 --> 00:02:22,615
and briefings export to Markdown or PDF.

38
00:02:23,520 --> 00:02:27,960
The mission agent picks its tools, runs them
in parallel, and streams an answer.

39
00:02:28,010 --> 00:02:31,159
Every number it states is checked against a
tool result, and the provenance drawer shows

40
00:02:31,209 --> 00:02:34,358
where each one came from.

41
00:02:35,263 --> 00:02:40,545
Ask the corpus, and every sentence carries a
citation, in English or in Bangla.

42
00:02:40,595 --> 00:02:45,000
Retrieval previews live, and new documents are
indexed in seconds.

43
00:02:45,905 --> 00:02:48,111
Pull the wifi, and it still works:

44
00:02:48,161 --> 00:02:51,196
every source falls back to a verified cache,
and Open Data still searches every Mars

45
00:02:51,246 --> 00:02:54,280
dataset.

46
00:02:55,185 --> 00:02:56,886
Earth gets its own lane:

47
00:02:56,936 --> 00:03:00,459
live EONET wildfires and storms on NASA's own
satellite imagery, never mixed into a Mars

48
00:03:00,509 --> 00:03:04,033
decision.

49
00:03:04,938 --> 00:03:07,608
Every page tells a 3D data story:

50
00:03:07,658 --> 00:03:13,996
Earth and Mars at true scale, Jezero cell by
cell, the traverse by sol, every NASA landing,

51
00:03:14,046 --> 00:03:20,384
slopes in relief, and a route raised into an
elevation curtain.

52
00:03:21,289 --> 00:03:23,292
Every page adds to the story:

53
00:03:23,342 --> 00:03:28,707
Earth widgets, the source register, targets, a
guided tour, run traces, the architecture, a

54
00:03:28,757 --> 00:03:34,123
gallery, model comparisons, Earth analogs and
a mission card.

55
00:03:36,028 --> 00:03:42,996
It's open source under Apache 2.0, with all AI
use disclosed, built for challenge eleven.

56
00:03:43,046 --> 00:03:47,018
Next, we need science mentors and classroom
pilots.

57
00:03:47,923 --> 00:03:51,316
One day, the first crew could rehearse their
Marswalk right here, with NASA's evidence at

58
00:03:51,366 --> 00:03:54,759
every step.

59
00:03:54,809 --> 00:03:55,850
Mars Explorer...

60
00:03:55,900 --> 00:03:57,350
from Binary Explorers.
